window.ALFORD_WALK = {"mapLabels":{"labels":[{"id":"jacob","p":[1070,-180],"title":"Jacob Alford’s heirs","sub":"The river claim · 1831","class":"jacob"},{"id":"john","p":[-140,3710],"title":"John S. Alford","sub":"Patents · 1858","class":"john"},{"id":"seaborn","p":[770,5510],"title":"Seaborn L. Alford","sub":"Homestead · 1881","class":"seaborn"},{"id":"river","p":[3850,2200],"title":"Bogue Chitto","sub":"","class":"river-label"},{"id":"riverSouth","p":[2570,4850],"title":"Bogue Chitto","sub":"","class":"river-label"}],"records":["landscape","survey","patents","homestead"],"body":"Historical land reconstructed on modern river geography.","html":"<p>Historical land reconstructed on modern river geography.<\/p>"},"scenes":[{"id":"beginning","year":"1768–2008","title":"The Alford family","visual":"map","records":["landscape"],"people":["christine"],"part":0,"hero":true,"body":"A family history through Christine Alford Knight. Follow the early records, the generations who farmed beside the Bogue Chitto, and Christine’s work as a nurse.\n\nMaps show where the records place them. Select a name to meet the person, or a document to read it.","html":"<p>A family history through Christine Alford Knight. Follow the early records, the generations who farmed beside the Bogue Chitto, and Christine’s work as a nurse.<\/p>\n<p>Maps show where the records place them. Select a name to meet the person, or a document to read it.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"north-carolina","year":"1768","title":"Julius Alford’s will","visual":"regional","records":["will"],"people":[],"part":1,"place":"North Carolina","region":"NC","regionLabel":"Modern Franklin County area · plantation unlocated","body":"Julius Alford left his son Jacob the home plantation on both sides of the Tar River in North Carolina. His will was written in 1768 and proved in 1771.\n\nThis is a possible beginning for Christine’s Louisiana line. The connection between this Jacob and the later Louisiana family remains unproved.","html":"<p>Julius Alford left his son Jacob the home plantation on both sides of the Tar River in North Carolina. His will was written in 1768 and proved in 1771.<\/p>\n<p>This is a possible beginning for Christine’s Louisiana line. The connection between this Jacob and the later Louisiana family remains unproved.<\/p>"},{"id":"will","year":"1768","title":"Land on the Tar River","visual":"image","records":["will"],"people":[],"image":"will","quote":"the Land & Plantation whereon I Now Live Lying on both Sides [Tar] River","quoteLabel":"Excerpt · Julius Alford’s will","place":"North Carolina","body":"The will names Jacob as Julius’s son. The handwriting is faint, so the relevant phrase is transcribed beside the page.\n\nThe plantation’s boundaries have not been located. The preceding map identifies the modern Franklin County area.","html":"<p>The will names Jacob as Julius’s son. The handwriting is faint, so the relevant phrase is transcribed beside the page.<\/p>\n<p>The plantation’s boundaries have not been located. The preceding map identifies the modern Franklin County area.<\/p>"},{"id":"deed","year":"1789","title":"Jacob identifies his father","visual":"image","records":["deed"],"people":[],"image":"deed","place":"Franklin County, North Carolina","roi":[2260,235,4160,750],"detailImage":true,"body":"In a deed twenty-one years later, Jacob called himself “the son of Julius Alford deceased.” This confirms the North Carolina relationship.\n\nIt does not yet establish a link to the Jacob who later claimed land in Louisiana.","html":"<p>In a deed twenty-one years later, Jacob called himself “the son of Julius Alford deceased.” This confirms the North Carolina relationship.<\/p>\n<p>It does not yet establish a link to the Jacob who later claimed land in Louisiana.<\/p>"},{"id":"south-carolina","year":"Before 1807","title":"The South Carolina account","visual":"regional","records":["twin","census1880"],"people":[],"place":"South Carolina","region":"SC","regionLabel":"South Carolina · no locality named in the account","body":"An obituary published in 1884 says Jacob Alford came from South Carolina shortly before the birth of his twin sons. It names no town or county.\n\nThis account may describe an earlier residence of the Louisiana family. It was written roughly seventy-five years after the events.","html":"<p>An obituary published in 1884 says Jacob Alford came from South Carolina shortly before the birth of his twin sons. It names no town or county.<\/p>\n<p>This account may describe an earlier residence of the Louisiana family. It was written roughly seventy-five years after the events.<\/p>"},{"id":"twins","year":"1807","title":"John and Seaborn","visual":"twins","records":["twin"],"people":["john"],"place":"Near modern Osyka","cards":[["TWIN · 1807","John Seaborn","Christine’s great-grandfather"],["TWIN · 1807","Seaborn John","His brother; the subject of the 1884 obituary"]],"graphicNote":"Two brothers with similar names. The younger Seaborn L. belongs to the next generation.","body":"John Seaborn and Seaborn John Alford were twins, born on 11 October 1807 according to Seaborn’s later obituary. It places their birth in Louisiana near the site of modern Osyka, Mississippi.\n\nJohn Seaborn is the brother followed here. He was Christine’s great-grandfather. The exact birth site is unknown.","html":"<p>John Seaborn and Seaborn John Alford were twins, born on 11 October 1807 according to Seaborn’s later obituary. It places their birth in Louisiana near the site of modern Osyka, Mississippi.<\/p>\n<p>John Seaborn is the brother followed here. He was Christine’s great-grandfather. The exact birth site is unknown.<\/p>"},{"id":"river","year":"1831","title":"The Bogue Chitto claim","visual":"claim","records":["survey"],"people":[],"body":"Land claimed by Jacob Alford was surveyed for his heirs in 1831. It lay beside the Bogue Chitto River in Louisiana.\n\nThis claimant may have been the twins’ father. Neither the heirs’ names nor a connection to the North Carolina Jacob is established by this survey.","html":"<p>Land claimed by Jacob Alford was surveyed for his heirs in 1831. It lay beside the Bogue Chitto River in Louisiana.<\/p>\n<p>This claimant may have been the twins’ father. Neither the heirs’ names nor a connection to the North Carolina Jacob is established by this survey.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"survey","year":"1831","title":"The original survey","visual":"image","records":["survey"],"people":[],"image":"survey","body":"The survey was dated 20 January 1831. Its small drawing places the river along the eastern side of the tract.\n\nThe map behind it uses a later reconstruction of the claim. The original drawing is kept in its own proportions.","html":"<p>The survey was dated 20 January 1831. Its small drawing places the river along the eastern side of the tract.<\/p>\n<p>The map behind it uses a later reconstruction of the claim. The original drawing is kept in its own proportions.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"heirs","year":"1831","title":"“Heirs Jacob Alford”","visual":"image","records":["survey"],"people":[],"image":"survey","roi":[720,475,1890,1140],"quiet":true,"detailImage":true,"body":"The name appears inside the drawing. Below, the survey records 636 acres and 54 hundredths: 636.54 acres.\n\nSelect the image to examine the entire sheet, or keep scrolling to follow the next generation.","html":"<p>The name appears inside the drawing. Below, the survey records 636 acres and 54 hundredths: 636.54 acres.<\/p>\n<p>Select the image to examine the entire sheet, or keep scrolling to follow the next generation.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"marriage","year":"1833","title":"John and Margaret","visual":"family","records":["margaret","parentage"],"people":["john","margaret"],"family":["john","margaret","seaborn"],"part":2,"familyCaption":"Christine’s great-grandparents","childLabel":"THEIR SON · BORN 1844","body":"John Seaborn Alford married Margaret Brumfield in 1833, according to her church obituary. Its dates make Margaret about fourteen at the time.\n\nCensus households and later family records support them as the parents of Seaborn L. Alford. They were Christine’s great-grandparents.","html":"<p>John Seaborn Alford married Margaret Brumfield in 1833, according to her church obituary. Its dates make Margaret about fourteen at the time.<\/p>\n<p>Census households and later family records support them as the parents of Seaborn L. Alford. They were Christine’s great-grandparents.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"son","year":"1844","title":"Their son, Seaborn L.","visual":"family","records":["parentage","seabornObit"],"people":["seaborn"],"family":["john","margaret","seaborn"],"child":true,"familyCaption":"Christine’s great-grandparents","childLabel":"THEIR SON · BORN 1844","body":"Seaborn L. Alford was born on 19 May 1844, according to his obituary. He appears as a child in John and Margaret’s household.\n\nHe is a different person from his uncle Seaborn John, the twin born in 1807. This younger Seaborn would become Christine’s grandfather.","html":"<p>Seaborn L. Alford was born on 19 May 1844, according to his obituary. He appears as a child in John and Margaret’s household.<\/p>\n<p>He is a different person from his uncle Seaborn John, the twin born in 1807. This younger Seaborn would become Christine’s grandfather.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"farm","year":"1850","title":"A working farm","visual":"farm","records":["farm"],"people":[],"graphicLabels":["40 improved acres","600 unimproved acres","One square = 10 acres · reported acreage, not a parcel map"],"body":"The agricultural return associated with John Alford lists forty improved acres and six hundred unimproved acres.\n\nThese figures describe the farm reported to the census. They do not locate its boundaries, establish ownership or identify it as Jacob’s earlier claim.","html":"<p>The agricultural return associated with John Alford lists forty improved acres and six hundred unimproved acres.<\/p>\n<p>These figures describe the farm reported to the census. They do not locate its boundaries, establish ownership or identify it as Jacob’s earlier claim.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"township","year":"1851","title":"The township plat","visual":"image","records":["plat","field"],"people":[],"image":"plat","body":"This government map divides the township into older claims and smaller numbered lots. The lot numbers and acreages allow later Alford patents to be placed.\n\nThe word FIELD appears beside an older claim boundary.","html":"<p>This government map divides the township into older claims and smaller numbered lots. The lot numbers and acreages allow later Alford patents to be placed.<\/p>\n<p>The word FIELD appears beside an older claim boundary.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"field","year":"1851","title":"A field before John’s patent","visual":"image","records":["field"],"people":[],"image":"plat","roi":[115,625,565,935],"quiet":true,"detailImage":true,"body":"The drawn field extends into lot 1 of section 18. Seven years later, John’s patent would include that lot.\n\nThe map does not name the person farming the field in 1851.","html":"<p>The drawn field extends into lot 1 of section 18. Seven years later, John’s patent would include that lot.<\/p>\n<p>The map does not name the person farming the field in 1851.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"john-land","year":"1858","title":"John’s land patents","visual":"john","records":["patents","field","landscape"],"people":["john"],"body":"Two patents dated 1 June 1858 recorded 93.84 acres in sections 17 and 18. The green areas show those separate lots.\n\nA family cemetery is later located within the section 18 lot. It is distinct from the cemetery beside Bogue Chitto church, where Esco would be buried.","html":"<p>Two patents dated 1 June 1858 recorded 93.84 acres in sections 17 and 18. The green areas show those separate lots.<\/p>\n<p>A family cemetery is later located within the section 18 lot. It is distinct from the cemetery beside Bogue Chitto church, where Esco would be buried.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"war","year":"1862","title":"Seaborn enlists","visual":"image","records":["muster","parole","portHudsonWorks"],"people":["seaborn"],"image":"portHudsonWorks","place":"Camp Moore · Port Hudson","roi":[420,315,2750,2690],"body":"Seaborn enlisted at Camp Moore on 13 May 1862, just before his eighteenth birthday. He served in the Confederate 9th Battalion Louisiana Partisan Rangers, later the 3rd Louisiana Cavalry.\n\nHis service cards record capture at Port Hudson in July 1863, followed by parole.","html":"<p>Seaborn enlisted at Camp Moore on 13 May 1862, just before his eighteenth birthday. He served in the Confederate 9th Battalion Louisiana Partisan Rangers, later the 3rd Louisiana Cavalry.<\/p>\n<p>His service cards record capture at Port Hudson in July 1863, followed by parole.<\/p>"},{"id":"port-hudson","year":"1863","title":"Port Hudson","visual":"image","records":["portHudsonBattery","parole"],"people":[],"image":"portHudsonBattery","place":"Port Hudson, Louisiana","roi":[240,340,2650,2680],"quiet":true,"body":"This photograph shows Union artillery during the siege. These were the opposing forces; no person pictured is identified as an Alford.\n\nSeaborn’s card records capture on 9 July. The held service records place his parole on 12–13 July, without settling a single day.","html":"<p>This photograph shows Union artillery during the siege. These were the opposing forces; no person pictured is identified as an Alford.<\/p>\n<p>Seaborn’s card records capture on 9 July. The held service records place his parole on 12–13 July, without settling a single day.<\/p>"},{"id":"laura","year":"1866","title":"Seaborn and Laura","visual":"family","records":["seabornObit","escoParentage","laura"],"people":["seaborn","laura"],"family":["seaborn","laura","esco"],"familyCaption":"Christine’s grandparents","body":"Seaborn married Laura A. Ellzy on 24 December 1866, according to his later obituary. Her surname is usually spelled Ellzey.\n\nThe obituary reports ten children from their marriage. Household and family records support Laura as the mother of Esco, the son followed here.","html":"<p>Seaborn married Laura A. Ellzy on 24 December 1866, according to his later obituary. Her surname is usually spelled Ellzey.<\/p>\n<p>The obituary reports ten children from their marriage. Household and family records support Laura as the mother of Esco, the son followed here.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"esco","year":"1878","title":"Esco Seaborn Alford","visual":"image","records":["escoDraft","escoParentage"],"people":["esco"],"image":"escoDraft","body":"Esco’s own draft registration gives his birth as 21 May 1878. In 1880, the census records “Escoe,” age two, as Seaborn’s son.\n\nHe was Christine’s father. His draft registration is a record of registration, not evidence that he served in the military.","html":"<p>Esco’s own draft registration gives his birth as 21 May 1878. In 1880, the census records “Escoe,” age two, as Seaborn’s son.<\/p>\n<p>He was Christine’s father. His draft registration is a record of registration, not evidence that he served in the military.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"homestead","year":"1881","title":"Seaborn’s homestead","visual":"seaborn","records":["homestead","patents","landscape"],"people":["seaborn"],"body":"On 10 February 1881, Seaborn received a homestead patent for 135.24 acres. His three lots in section 20 lay south of his father’s earlier lots.\n\nThe retained outlines compare dated land records; they do not imply continuous ownership. Neither house has been located.","html":"<p>On 10 February 1881, Seaborn received a homestead patent for 135.24 acres. His three lots in section 20 lay south of his father’s earlier lots.<\/p>\n<p>The retained outlines compare dated land records; they do not imply continuous ownership. Neither house has been located.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"margaret","year":"1885","title":"Margaret’s church life","visual":"image","records":["margaret"],"people":["margaret"],"image":"margaret","body":"Margaret joined the Methodists in 1853 and was baptized at Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866. She later transferred her membership to Bogue Chitto.\n\nHer church’s obituary records these affiliations and her death on 8 September 1885. It says she left John, fourteen children and forty-three grandchildren.","html":"<p>Margaret joined the Methodists in 1853 and was baptized at Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866. She later transferred her membership to Bogue Chitto.<\/p>\n<p>Her church’s obituary records these affiliations and her death on 8 September 1885. It says she left John, fourteen children and forty-three grandchildren.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"mary-lou","year":"1899","title":"Esco and Mary Lou","visual":"family","records":["escoMarriage","maryObit","census1900"],"people":["esco","mary-lou"],"family":["esco","mary-lou","christine"],"part":3,"familyCaption":"Christine’s parents","childLabel":"THEIR DAUGHTER · ABOUT 1921","body":"The marriage index records Esco Alford and Mary L. Fussell on 28 December 1899. Mary Lou also appears as Lou in the records. They became Christine’s parents.\n\nThe records support Henry T. Fussell as her father. Eliza or Liza Hobbs is her likely mother, though the indexes disagree on the mother’s name.","html":"<p>The marriage index records Esco Alford and Mary L. Fussell on 28 December 1899. Mary Lou also appears as Lou in the records. They became Christine’s parents.<\/p>\n<p>The records support Henry T. Fussell as her father. Eliza or Liza Hobbs is her likely mother, though the indexes disagree on the mother’s name.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"households","year":"1900","title":"Separate households, nearby","visual":"households","records":["census1900"],"people":["esco","mary-lou"],"cards":[["THE OLDER GENERATION","Seaborn & Laura","Esco’s parents"],["A SEPARATE HOUSEHOLD","Esco & Mary Lou","Married in 1899"]],"graphicNote":"Related households in the census sequence. This diagram does not locate or space their homes.","body":"The 1900 census lists Esco and Mary Lou on a farm that he owned. His father and brother appear nearby in the enumerator’s sequence.\n\nThe sequence suggests proximity. It does not give distances, house sites or the boundaries of their farms.","html":"<p>The 1900 census lists Esco and Mary Lou on a farm that he owned. His father and brother appear nearby in the enumerator’s sequence.<\/p>\n<p>The sequence suggests proximity. It does not give distances, house sites or the boundaries of their farms.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"laura-death","year":"1903","title":"Laura’s death","visual":"image","records":["laura","seabornObit"],"people":["laura"],"image":"laura","body":"A parish cemetery transcription gives Laura’s death as 19 March 1903, after more than thirty-six years of marriage to Seaborn.\n\nHer gravestone is weathered, and the dates cannot be read reliably in this photograph. Esco had already married and established his own household.","html":"<p>A parish cemetery transcription gives Laura’s death as 19 March 1903, after more than thirty-six years of marriage to Seaborn.<\/p>\n<p>Her gravestone is weathered, and the dates cannot be read reliably in this photograph. Esco had already married and established his own household.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"candacy","year":"1909","title":"Seaborn’s second marriage","visual":"timeline","records":["seabornObit","candacyObit"],"people":["seaborn"],"milestones":[["1866","Marriage to Laura","Ten children reported"],["1909","Marriage to Candacy","Three children reported"]],"body":"Seaborn married Candacy Bankston on 8 September 1909, according to his obituary. His obituary reports three children from this marriage. Candacy’s 1955 obituary names Jacob, Frankie and Agnes.\n\nThey were Esco’s younger half-siblings. The family therefore included children from two marriages, separated by a considerable span of years.","html":"<p>Seaborn married Candacy Bankston on 8 September 1909, according to his obituary. His obituary reports three children from this marriage. Candacy’s 1955 obituary names Jacob, Frankie and Agnes.<\/p>\n<p>They were Esco’s younger half-siblings. The family therefore included children from two marriages, separated by a considerable span of years.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"seaborn-death","year":"1919","title":"Seaborn dies near Warnerton","visual":"all-land","records":["seabornObit","landscape"],"people":["seaborn"],"body":"Seaborn died at home on 22 November 1919, three miles south of Warnerton. His obituary says he died “in sight of the place of his birth.”\n\nFrom the centre of Jacob’s claim to Seaborn’s homestead is about three and a half miles. The family’s land records cover several sections of the same township.","html":"<p>Seaborn died at home on 22 November 1919, three miles south of Warnerton. His obituary says he died “in sight of the place of his birth.”<\/p>\n<p>From the centre of Jacob’s claim to Seaborn’s homestead is about three and a half miles. The family’s land records cover several sections of the same township.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"christine","year":"1921","title":"Christine Alford","visual":"family","records":["memorial","census1930"],"people":["christine"],"family":["esco","mary-lou","christine"],"child":true,"place":"Mount Hermon, Louisiana","familyCaption":"Christine’s parents","childLabel":"THEIR DAUGHTER · ABOUT 1921","body":"Christine was born to Esco and Mary Lou about 1921. Her later obituary gives 1 January 1921 and a farm at Mount Hermon as her birthplace. An original birth record has not been reached.\n\nThrough her father, she was Seaborn and Laura’s granddaughter and John and Margaret’s great-granddaughter.","html":"<p>Christine was born to Esco and Mary Lou about 1921. Her later obituary gives 1 January 1921 and a farm at Mount Hermon as her birthplace. An original birth record has not been reached.<\/p>\n<p>Through her father, she was Seaborn and Laura’s granddaughter and John and Margaret’s great-granddaughter.<\/p>"},{"id":"census","year":"1930","title":"Christine, age nine","visual":"image","records":["census1930","maryObit"],"people":["christine"],"image":"census1930","roi":[280,230,1560,747],"detailImage":true,"body":"The census lists Esco, Lou, Tolbert and Christine in their Washington Parish household. Esco was a farmer. Tolbert was sixteen; Christine was nine and attending school.\n\nThe relationship column identifies Christine as Esco’s daughter. Mary Lou’s own obituary later names Christine as her daughter as well.","html":"<p>The census lists Esco, Lou, Tolbert and Christine in their Washington Parish household. Esco was a farmer. Tolbert was sixteen; Christine was nine and attending school.<\/p>\n<p>The relationship column identifies Christine as Esco’s daughter. Mary Lou’s own obituary later names Christine as her daughter as well.<\/p>","place":"Bogue Chitto, Louisiana"},{"id":"touro","year":"1941","title":"Nursing school in New Orleans","visual":"image","records":["nursing","touroPhoto"],"people":["christine"],"image":"touroPhoto","part":4,"place":"New Orleans, Louisiana","body":"Christine graduated from Touro Infirmary’s school of nursing on 20 August 1941.\n\nThis photograph shows Touro around 1910, about thirty years before her graduation. It provides a view of the institution, not a photograph of her class.","html":"<p>Christine graduated from Touro Infirmary’s school of nursing on 20 August 1941.<\/p>\n<p>This photograph shows Touro around 1910, about thirty years before her graduation. It provides a view of the institution, not a photograph of her class.<\/p>"},{"id":"graduation","year":"1941","title":"Esco brings her home","visual":"image","records":["nursing"],"people":["esco","christine"],"image":"nursing","place":"New Orleans · Mount Hermon","roi":[1045,1490,1410,1730],"detailImage":true,"body":"The hometown paper reported that Christine would spend three weeks with her parents before returning to Touro to nurse. In the next item, it reported that Esco had driven to New Orleans to bring her home.\n\nThe notice appears in the “Mt. Hermon Events” column on 29 August 1941.","html":"<p>The hometown paper reported that Christine would spend three weeks with her parents before returning to Touro to nurse. In the next item, it reported that Esco had driven to New Orleans to bring her home.<\/p>\n<p>The notice appears in the “Mt. Hermon Events” column on 29 August 1941.<\/p>"},{"id":"baton-rouge","year":"1942–1943","title":"A public-health nurse","visual":"cities","records":["directory","nursing"],"people":["christine"],"place":"Baton Rouge, Louisiana","locations":[{"name":"MOUNT HERMON","year":"Childhood","role":"Family community","coordinates":[-90.2959307,30.9601859]},{"name":"NEW ORLEANS","year":"1941","role":"Touro nursing school","coordinates":[-90.092608,29.924923]},{"name":"BATON ROUGE","year":"1942–1943","role":"Parish Health Unit","coordinates":[-91.175538,30.418439]}],"graphicNote":"Community and city reference points, not exact homes or offices. Dotted lines connect the places in the account; they do not trace a known travel route.","body":"The 1942 and 1943 Baton Rouge directories list Christine Alford as a nurse with the Parish Health Unit.\n\nHer records now connect the family’s Mount Hermon community with New Orleans, where she trained, and Baton Rouge, where she worked.","html":"<p>The 1942 and 1943 Baton Rouge directories list Christine Alford as a nurse with the Parish Health Unit.<\/p>\n<p>Her records now connect the family’s Mount Hermon community with New Orleans, where she trained, and Baton Rouge, where she worked.<\/p>"},{"id":"peabody","year":"1944","title":"Further training","visual":"timeline","records":["memorial","nursing","directory"],"people":["christine"],"milestones":[["1941","Touro Infirmary","School of nursing"],["1942–1943","Parish Health Unit","Nurse in Baton Rouge"],["1944","Peabody College","CPH, reported in her obituary"]],"place":"Education & work","body":"Christine’s later obituary reports a CPH qualification from Peabody College in 1944. It calls this a “CPH degree”; a transcript or contemporary graduation notice has not been examined.\n\nThe earlier newspaper and directories independently document her nursing graduation and public-health employment.","html":"<p>Christine’s later obituary reports a CPH qualification from Peabody College in 1944. It calls this a “CPH degree”; a transcript or contemporary graduation notice has not been examined.<\/p>\n<p>The earlier newspaper and directories independently document her nursing graduation and public-health employment.<\/p>"},{"id":"christine-marriage","year":"1946","title":"Christine and Charles Knight","visual":"family","records":["marriage","census1950"],"people":["christine","charles"],"family":["christine","charles"],"place":"Bienville Parish, Louisiana","body":"The Louisiana marriage index records Christine Alford and Charles B. Knight on 1 February 1946 in Bienville Parish. Other records give his full name as Charles Binford Knight.\n\nShe appears thereafter as Christine Alford Knight, Christine A. Knight and Mrs. C. B. Knight.","html":"<p>The Louisiana marriage index records Christine Alford and Charles B. Knight on 1 February 1946 in Bienville Parish. Other records give his full name as Charles Binford Knight.<\/p>\n<p>She appears thereafter as Christine Alford Knight, Christine A. Knight and Mrs. C. B. Knight.<\/p>"},{"id":"mary-lou-death","year":"1946","title":"Mary Lou’s death","visual":"image","records":["maryObit","marriage"],"people":["mary-lou"],"image":"maryObit","place":"Mount Hermon, Louisiana","body":"Mary Lou died at home near Mount Hermon on 4 February 1946, three days after Christine’s indexed marriage date. Her newspaper obituary names her daughter as “Mrs. Christine Knight.”\n\nIt names Esco and the adult children and schedules services at Bogue Chitto church. It describes Christine as of Mount Hermon, but does not establish that she was present when her mother died.","html":"<p>Mary Lou died at home near Mount Hermon on 4 February 1946, three days after Christine’s indexed marriage date. Her newspaper obituary names her daughter as “Mrs. Christine Knight.”<\/p>\n<p>It names Esco and the adult children and schedules services at Bogue Chitto church. It describes Christine as of Mount Hermon, but does not establish that she was present when her mother died.<\/p>"},{"id":"lynnie","year":"1946–1947","title":"Esco remarries","visual":"timeline","records":["escoMarriage"],"people":["esco"],"milestones":[["4 February 1946","Mary Lou dies","Christine’s mother"],["30 April 1946","Esco marries Lynnie","Date in the marriage index"],["1947","Lynnie dies","A second bereavement"]],"place":"Louisiana","body":"A compiled marriage index records Esco’s marriage to Lynnie Schilling on 30 April 1946. She died in 1947.\n\nThis later marriage helps explain why a 1947 record refers to Mrs. E. S. Alford after Mary Lou’s death.","html":"<p>A compiled marriage index records Esco’s marriage to Lynnie Schilling on 30 April 1946. She died in 1947.<\/p>\n<p>This later marriage helps explain why a 1947 record refers to Mrs. E. S. Alford after Mary Lou’s death.<\/p>"},{"id":"adult-household","year":"1950","title":"Christine at work","visual":"adult-census","records":["census1950","directory"],"people":["christine","charles"],"place":"Baton Rouge, Louisiana","graphicLabel":"1950 CENSUS · ADULT ENTRIES","adults":[["Christine A. Knight","Registered nurse · parish health","Government employment · 40 hours"],["Charles B. Knight","Assistant manager · funeral home","Baton Rouge, Louisiana"]],"graphicNote":"Selected adult entries transcribed. The full page, which includes a living descendant, is not reproduced.","body":"The 1950 census records Christine and Charles in Baton Rouge. She was a registered nurse working in parish health; he was an assistant manager at a funeral home.\n\nHer entry reports a forty-hour workweek and government employment.","html":"<p>The 1950 census records Christine and Charles in Baton Rouge. She was a registered nurse working in parish health; he was an assistant manager at a funeral home.<\/p>\n<p>Her entry reports a forty-hour workweek and government employment.<\/p>"},{"id":"florence","year":"1951","title":"Esco and Florence Bennett","visual":"timeline","records":["escoMarriage","escoObit"],"people":["esco"],"quiet":true,"milestones":[["1899","Mary Lou Fussell","Died in 1946"],["1946","Lynnie Schilling","Died in 1947"],["1951","Florence Bennett","Later circumstances unresolved"]],"place":"Louisiana","body":"A marriage to Florence Bennett is indexed on 23 May 1951. Esco’s later obituary names no wife, and Florence’s subsequent circumstances remain unresolved.","html":"<p>A marriage to Florence Bennett is indexed on 23 May 1951. Esco’s later obituary names no wife, and Florence’s subsequent circumstances remain unresolved.<\/p>"},{"id":"esco-death","year":"1958","title":"Farmer, dairyman and deacon","visual":"image","records":["escoObit","escoPortrait"],"people":["esco"],"image":"escoPortrait","place":"Tylertown · Bogue Chitto","body":"Esco died at Tylertown Hospital after a stroke. The likely date is 15 April 1958, inferred from the newspaper’s issue date and its reference to Tuesday.\n\nThe obituary and a later portrait caption describe a retired farmer and dairyman, a church deacon and a parish jury commissioner. Christine Knight of Baton Rouge was named among his children. He was buried beside Bogue Chitto Baptist Church.","html":"<p>Esco died at Tylertown Hospital after a stroke. The likely date is 15 April 1958, inferred from the newspaper’s issue date and its reference to Tuesday.<\/p>\n<p>The obituary and a later portrait caption describe a retired farmer and dairyman, a church deacon and a parish jury commissioner. Christine Knight of Baton Rouge was named among his children. He was buried beside Bogue Chitto Baptist Church.<\/p>"},{"id":"retirement","year":"1975","title":"Thirty-three years in public health","visual":"timeline","records":["memorial","directory","census1950"],"people":["christine"],"part":5,"milestones":[["1942–1943","Parish Health Unit","Directory entries"],["1950","Registered nurse","Parish health · census entry"],["1975","Retirement","After 33 years, reported in obituary"]],"place":"Louisiana","body":"Christine retired in 1975 after thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department, according to her later obituary.\n\nHer work included coordinating clinics for children with disabilities across several parishes. Directories and the 1950 census document the earlier years of this public-health career.","html":"<p>Christine retired in 1975 after thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department, according to her later obituary.<\/p>\n<p>Her work included coordinating clinics for children with disabilities across several parishes. Directories and the 1950 census document the earlier years of this public-health career.<\/p>"},{"id":"christine-death","year":"2008","title":"Christine Alford Knight","visual":"lineage","records":["memorial","parentage","escoParentage","maryObit"],"people":["christine"],"place":"Christine’s family","graphicNote":"John and Margaret’s son Seaborn married Laura. Seaborn and Laura’s son Esco married Mary Lou. Esco and Mary Lou’s daughter was Christine. Select a name for the supporting records.","body":"Christine died in 2008. Her obituary records her nursing career, work with the Trinity Episcopal Church Altar Guild, and forty-four-year marriage to Charles, who predeceased her.\n\nIt names her parents as Esco Seaborn Alford and Mary Lou Fussell. The held accounts disagree on her exact death date, so this story gives the year only.","html":"<p>Christine died in 2008. Her obituary records her nursing career, work with the Trinity Episcopal Church Altar Guild, and forty-four-year marriage to Charles, who predeceased her.<\/p>\n<p>It names her parents as Esco Seaborn Alford and Mary Lou Fussell. The held accounts disagree on her exact death date, so this story gives the year only.<\/p>"}],"people":{"margaret":{"id":"margaret","name":"Margaret Brumfield Alford","dates":"1819–1885","relationship":"Christine’s great-grandmother","archiveKey":"margaret-brumfield","refs":"S-1572","records":["margaret","parentage"],"body":"Margaret married John S. Alford in 1833, according to her church obituary. Family and census records support them as Seaborn L.’s parents.\n\nHer maiden name, Brumfield, comes from later family compilations; her church obituary names her only as Margaret Alford.\n\nHer obituary records successive memberships in the Methodist church, Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church and Bogue Chitto church. At her death on 8 September 1885, it counted fourteen surviving children and forty-three grandchildren.","html":"<p>Margaret married John S. Alford in 1833, according to her church obituary. Family and census records support them as Seaborn L.’s parents.<\/p>\n<p>Her maiden name, Brumfield, comes from later family compilations; her church obituary names her only as Margaret Alford.<\/p>\n<p>Her obituary records successive memberships in the Methodist church, Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church and Bogue Chitto church. At her death on 8 September 1885, it counted fourteen surviving children and forty-three grandchildren.<\/p>"},"mary-lou":{"id":"mary-lou","name":"Mary Lou Fussell Alford","dates":"1883 or 1884–1946","relationship":"Christine’s mother","archiveKey":"mary-louisa-fussell","records":["escoMarriage","maryObit","census1930"],"body":"Mary Lou, often called Lou in the records, married Esco Alford on 28 December 1899, according to a compiled marriage index. Her own obituary names her daughter Christine Knight.\n\nThe records support Henry T. Fussell as her father. Eliza or Liza Hobbs is the likely mother, based on indexes that are not entirely consistent. Mary Lou died on 4 February 1946. Her birth year remains unresolved.","html":"<p>Mary Lou, often called Lou in the records, married Esco Alford on 28 December 1899, according to a compiled marriage index. Her own obituary names her daughter Christine Knight.<\/p>\n<p>The records support Henry T. Fussell as her father. Eliza or Liza Hobbs is the likely mother, based on indexes that are not entirely consistent. Mary Lou died on 4 February 1946. Her birth year remains unresolved.<\/p>"},"esco":{"id":"esco","name":"Esco Seaborn Alford","dates":"1878–1958","relationship":"Christine’s father","archiveKey":"P-045","records":["escoDraft","escoParentage","escoMarriage","escoObit"],"body":"Esco was a farmer and dairyman, the son of Seaborn L. and Laura Alford. His own draft card gives his birth as 21 May 1878.\n\nHe married Mary Lou Fussell in 1899. Their daughter Christine trained as a nurse. After Mary Lou died in 1946, Esco married Lynnie Schilling, who died in 1947, and later Florence Bennett. The later marriage dates come from compiled indexes. His death on 15 April 1958 is inferred from the newspaper’s issue date and weekday.","html":"<p>Esco was a farmer and dairyman, the son of Seaborn L. and Laura Alford. His own draft card gives his birth as 21 May 1878.<\/p>\n<p>He married Mary Lou Fussell in 1899. Their daughter Christine trained as a nurse. After Mary Lou died in 1946, Esco married Lynnie Schilling, who died in 1947, and later Florence Bennett. The later marriage dates come from compiled indexes. His death on 15 April 1958 is inferred from the newspaper’s issue date and weekday.<\/p>"},"christine":{"id":"christine","name":"Christine Alford Knight","dates":"About 1921–2008","relationship":"The descendant followed in this story","archiveKey":"P-044","records":["census1930","nursing","directory","marriage","census1950","memorial"],"body":"Christine was the daughter of Esco and Mary Lou Alford. She graduated from Touro Infirmary’s school of nursing in 1941 and worked in public health in Baton Rouge.\n\nHer marriage to Charles Binford Knight is indexed on 1 February 1946. Her later obituary reports retirement in 1975 after thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department. It gives her birth as 1 January 1921; an original birth record has not been reached. Only her death year, 2008, is used because the held accounts disagree on the exact date.","html":"<p>Christine was the daughter of Esco and Mary Lou Alford. She graduated from Touro Infirmary’s school of nursing in 1941 and worked in public health in Baton Rouge.<\/p>\n<p>Her marriage to Charles Binford Knight is indexed on 1 February 1946. Her later obituary reports retirement in 1975 after thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department. It gives her birth as 1 January 1921; an original birth record has not been reached. Only her death year, 2008, is used because the held accounts disagree on the exact date.<\/p>"},"laura":{"id":"laura","name":"Laura A. Ellzey Alford","dates":"1844–1903, reported","relationship":"Christine’s grandmother","archiveKey":"P-052","records":["laura","seabornObit","escoParentage"],"body":"Laura married Seaborn L. Alford on 24 December 1866, according to his later obituary. It reports ten children from their marriage; household and family evidence supports her as Esco’s mother.\n\nShe was probably the daughter of John and Elizabeth Ellzey. Her death on 19 March 1903 is reported by a cemetery transcription; the held photograph does not independently resolve the worn digits.","html":"<p>Laura married Seaborn L. Alford on 24 December 1866, according to his later obituary. It reports ten children from their marriage; household and family evidence supports her as Esco’s mother.<\/p>\n<p>She was probably the daughter of John and Elizabeth Ellzey. Her death on 19 March 1903 is reported by a cemetery transcription; the held photograph does not independently resolve the worn digits.<\/p>"},"charles":{"id":"charles","name":"Charles Binford Knight","dates":"Married 1946","relationship":"Christine’s husband","archiveKey":"P-018","records":["marriage","census1950","memorial"],"body":"Charles B. Knight married Christine Alford on 1 February 1946, according to the Louisiana marriage index. The 1950 census places them in Baton Rouge and records his work as an assistant manager at a funeral home.\n\nChristine’s obituary describes a forty-four-year marriage and says that he predeceased her.","html":"<p>Charles B. Knight married Christine Alford on 1 February 1946, according to the Louisiana marriage index. The 1950 census places them in Baton Rouge and records his work as an assistant manager at a funeral home.<\/p>\n<p>Christine’s obituary describes a forty-four-year marriage and says that he predeceased her.<\/p>"},"seaborn":{"id":"seaborn","name":"Seaborn L. Alford","dates":"1844–1919","relationship":"Christine’s grandfather","archiveKey":"P-051","records":["seabornObit","parentage","muster","homestead"],"body":"Seaborn was the son of John and Margaret, according to the combined census and family evidence. He served in the Confederate cavalry and was captured and paroled at Port Hudson in 1863.\n\nHe married Laura A. Ellzy, usually spelled Ellzey, in 1866. Esco was one of their children. After Laura’s death, he married Candacy Bankston in 1909; his obituary reports three children from that marriage.","html":"<p>Seaborn was the son of John and Margaret, according to the combined census and family evidence. He served in the Confederate cavalry and was captured and paroled at Port Hudson in 1863.<\/p>\n<p>He married Laura A. Ellzy, usually spelled Ellzey, in 1866. Esco was one of their children. After Laura’s death, he married Candacy Bankston in 1909; his obituary reports three children from that marriage.<\/p>"},"john":{"id":"john","name":"John Seaborn Alford","dates":"1807–1891 or 1897","relationship":"Christine’s great-grandfather","archiveKey":"john-seaborn-alford","records":["twin","parentage","margaret","patents"],"body":"John was Margaret Brumfield’s husband and a Washington Parish farmer. Census households and later family records support them as the parents of Seaborn L. Alford.\n\nHis twin brother’s obituary gives their birth as 11 October 1807 and names their father Jacob. It does not settle which Jacob. Later sources disagree about John’s death year.","html":"<p>John was Margaret Brumfield’s husband and a Washington Parish farmer. Census households and later family records support them as the parents of Seaborn L. Alford.<\/p>\n<p>His twin brother’s obituary gives their birth as 11 October 1807 and names their father Jacob. It does not settle which Jacob. Later sources disagree about John’s death year.<\/p>"}},"records":{"portHudsonCamp":{"id":"portHudsonCamp","title":"A camp at Port Hudson","citation":"McPherson & Oliver · May 1863–1865 · Library of Congress","refs":"LOC 2015647757 · LC-DIG-stereo-1s04334","image":"assets/port-hudson-camp.jpg","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2015647757/","caption":"Camp at Port Hudson · May 1863–1865 · McPherson & Oliver / Library of Congress","body":"The Library of Congress catalogs this stereograph as soldiers in camp during the siege of Port Hudson, with a date range of May 1863–1865. It shows figures, tents and camp equipment beneath trees. The catalog does not identify Seaborn Alford or his unit. Rights advisory: No known restrictions on publication.","html":"<p>The Library of Congress catalogs this stereograph as soldiers in camp during the siege of Port Hudson, with a date range of May 1863–1865. It shows figures, tents and camp equipment beneath trees. The catalog does not identify Seaborn Alford or his unit. Rights advisory: No known restrictions on publication.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/port-hudson-camp.jpg","dimensions":[5514,3043],"displayBytes":190167,"sourceSha256":"ecefcc87d99f0908d8c936bcea0f81daa3d3168c43bc7eefbc517b0b204486c1"},"census1930":{"id":"census1930","title":"The Alford household in 1930","citation":"Washington Parish · Ward 2 · ED 59-2 · sheet 2A, lines 19–22","refs":"S-1638; C-208","image":"assets/census-1930-excerpt.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Alford household · 1930 census · assembled excerpt with original headings","body":"Esco, 52; Lou, 47; Tolbert, 16; Christine, 9. Christine is called daughter and is attending school. Ages are reproduced as recorded, not recalculated from later birth dates.\n\nThe displayed exhibit combines the original page heading and the four Alford household rows (lines 19–22). Intervening and unrelated household rows are omitted. This is an assembled excerpt, not a continuous section of the page.","html":"<p>Esco, 52; Lou, 47; Tolbert, 16; Christine, 9. Christine is called daughter and is attending school. Ages are reproduced as recorded, not recalculated from later birth dates.<\/p>\n<p>The displayed exhibit combines the original page heading and the four Alford household rows (lines 19–22). Intervening and unrelated household rows are omitted. This is an assembled excerpt, not a continuous section of the page.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/census-1930-excerpt.jpg","dimensions":[3680,747],"displayBytes":124451,"sourceSha256":"4f16720562c8a6b3ae69973794774282ebdd133308dfcf8edfbd765ecad5ad4e"},"margaret":{"id":"margaret","title":"Margaret Alford’s obituary","citation":"Magee’s Creek Baptist Association minutes · 1885","refs":"S-1869; S-1826","image":"assets/margaret-obituary.jpeg","url":null,"caption":"Margaret Alford · Magee’s Creek Baptist Association minutes · 1885","body":"Margaret’s obituary appears in the 1885 minutes of the Magee’s Creek Baptist Association. It reports her birth in Washington Parish on 9 February 1819, marriage to John S. Alford in 1833, church memberships and death on 8 September 1885.\n\nShe joined the Methodists in 1853, was baptized into Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866, and later transferred to Bogue Chitto. The writer says she left a husband, fourteen children and forty-three grandchildren. These counts describe survivors in the church account; they are not a reconstructed list of all births.\n\nThe displayed extract includes the end of one page and the continuation on the next. The marriage and birth dates are retrospective statements, rather than civil registers.","html":"<p>Margaret’s obituary appears in the 1885 minutes of the Magee’s Creek Baptist Association. It reports her birth in Washington Parish on 9 February 1819, marriage to John S. Alford in 1833, church memberships and death on 8 September 1885.<\/p>\n<p>She joined the Methodists in 1853, was baptized into Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866, and later transferred to Bogue Chitto. The writer says she left a husband, fourteen children and forty-three grandchildren. These counts describe survivors in the church account; they are not a reconstructed list of all births.<\/p>\n<p>The displayed extract includes the end of one page and the continuation on the next. The marriage and birth dates are retrospective statements, rather than civil registers.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/margaret-obituary.jpg","dimensions":[539,679],"displayBytes":82255,"sourceSha256":"aa1641999dd3ccd970f9823d09c10513f5c2642bb61a7d025964b21d24a1416d"},"portHudsonBattery":{"id":"portHudsonBattery","title":"Union artillery at Port Hudson","citation":"McPherson & Oliver · 1863 · Library of Congress","refs":"LOC 2015647751 · LC-DIG-stereo-1s04328","image":"assets/port-hudson-battery.jpg","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2015647751/","caption":"Union artillery at Port Hudson · 1863 · McPherson & Oliver / Library of Congress","body":"The Library of Congress identifies this stereograph as Union sailors at Battery 10 during the siege of Port Hudson in May and June 1863. It shows artillery and people at the battery. It is a contextual view of the opposing forces, not a photograph of Seaborn Alford or his Confederate unit. Rights advisory: No known restrictions on publication.","html":"<p>The Library of Congress identifies this stereograph as Union sailors at Battery 10 during the siege of Port Hudson in May and June 1863. It shows artillery and people at the battery. It is a contextual view of the opposing forces, not a photograph of Seaborn Alford or his Confederate unit. Rights advisory: No known restrictions on publication.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/port-hudson-battery.jpg","dimensions":[5409,2959],"displayBytes":174590,"sourceSha256":"1edd130eb9fdceb02a735182bd14bd17ef2456974145dde7d7d44e8b13372d4e"},"census1900":{"id":"census1900","title":"Esco and Mary Lou’s 1900 household","citation":"Washington Parish · 1900 federal census","refs":"S-1292; alford-direct-line-land-tenure-2026-09-08/findings.md","image":null,"url":null,"body":"Esco is recorded owning his farm. The page’s mortgage column cannot be relied on in 1900, so this story does not say whether the farm was mortgaged; that column can first be trusted in 1910, when it shows Esco’s farm free of mortgage. Mary L. is recorded born March 1883. The father’s and brother’s households occur nearby in the census sequence; the exact house or farm boundaries are not established by that sequence.","html":"<p>Esco is recorded owning his farm. The page’s mortgage column cannot be relied on in 1900, so this story does not say whether the farm was mortgaged; that column can first be trusted in 1910, when it shows Esco’s farm free of mortgage. Mary L. is recorded born March 1883. The father’s and brother’s households occur nearby in the census sequence; the exact house or farm boundaries are not established by that sequence.<\/p>"},"seabornObit":{"id":"seabornObit","title":"“The End Of A Long And Useful Life”","citation":"The Era-Leader · 4 December 1919, page 2","refs":"S-1654; C-211; C-212","image":"assets/seaborn-obituary-1919.png","url":null,"caption":"Seaborn L. Alford’s obituary · The Era-Leader · 4 December 1919","body":"An obituary by W. F. Hutson records birth on 19 May 1844, marriage to Laura A. Ellzy on 24 December 1866, ten children by Laura, a second marriage in 1909, and death on 22 November 1919. The full newspaper page is preserved here.","html":"<p>An obituary by W. F. Hutson records birth on 19 May 1844, marriage to Laura A. Ellzy on 24 December 1866, ten children by Laura, a second marriage in 1909, and death on 22 November 1919. The full newspaper page is preserved here.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/seaborn-obituary-1919.jpg","dimensions":[1600,2712],"displayBytes":483629,"sourceSha256":"e5b7d9104c2e6210709a8e5bad2f97b91638c7a4e7ca24477aa75deba99aa4c5"},"census1950":{"id":"census1950","title":"Christine and Charles in the 1950 census","citation":"Baton Rouge · adult household entries · image 18, lines 15–16","refs":"S-1861","image":null,"url":null,"body":"Charles B., 33, is an assistant manager at a funeral home. Christine A., 29, is a registered nurse working as a parish health nurse, 40 hours, government employment. This display transcribes adult details only; the page also includes a living descendant and is not reproduced.","html":"<p>Charles B., 33, is an assistant manager at a funeral home. Christine A., 29, is a registered nurse working as a parish health nurse, 40 hours, government employment. This display transcribes adult details only; the page also includes a living descendant and is not reproduced.<\/p>"},"muster":{"id":"muster","title":"S. L. Alford’s muster card","citation":"National Archives · M320 · 9th Battalion Louisiana Partisan Rangers","refs":"S-1808; S-1809","image":"assets/muster-1862.jpg","url":null,"body":"The compiled service record gives enlistment on 13 May 1862 at Camp Moore, Company K. The battalion later became Wingfield’s 3rd Louisiana Cavalry. This was Confederate service.","html":"<p>The compiled service record gives enlistment on 13 May 1862 at Camp Moore, Company K. The battalion later became Wingfield’s 3rd Louisiana Cavalry. This was Confederate service.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/muster-1862.jpg","dimensions":[1196,2685],"displayBytes":90178,"sourceSha256":"594da964a8d85d26f246d0f098e82a9853bfe14a03837a58739a03b77bf41a34"},"parole":{"id":"parole","title":"Port Hudson prisoner-of-war card","citation":"National Archives · captured 9 July 1863; paroled 12–13 July","refs":"S-1808; S-1809","image":"assets/parole-1863.jpg","url":null,"body":"The card in S. L. Alford’s service jacket records capture and parole at Port Hudson. It is a compiled service record, not a photograph of the original parole roll.","html":"<p>The card in S. L. Alford’s service jacket records capture and parole at Port Hudson. It is a compiled service record, not a photograph of the original parole roll.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/parole-1863.jpg","dimensions":[1196,2638],"displayBytes":88081,"sourceSha256":"28a89a25561b25e97f5b0812fc84b5d7e84610f9bdb9bec7aaa9ab9d2088187f"},"will":{"id":"will","title":"Julius Alford’s will","citation":"Bute County, North Carolina · written 14 July 1768, proved November 1771","refs":"C-139","image":"assets/julius-will-1768.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Julius Alford’s will · written 1768, proved 1771 · FamilySearch","body":"The manuscript names son Jacob and the home plantation on both sides of the Tar River. The image is a held will page, not a survey.","html":"<p>The manuscript names son Jacob and the home plantation on both sides of the Tar River. The image is a held will page, not a survey.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/julius-will-1768.jpg","dimensions":[3073,4554],"displayBytes":298812,"sourceSha256":"3c988d0d8f67cdd39cb4a6eb3fd8565b7747293cdef51e9907165a1811af1d0c"},"johnPortrait":{"id":"johnPortrait","title":"Portrait attributed to John Seaborn Alford","citation":"AAFA attribution · photograph undated","refs":"S-1869","image":"assets/john-portrait.jpg","url":null,"body":"Uploaded to John Seaborn’s Find a Grave memorial by Pamela Thompson in 2013, credited to the Alford American Family Association archives. The photograph itself does not name its subject.","html":"<p>Uploaded to John Seaborn’s Find a Grave memorial by Pamela Thompson in 2013, credited to the Alford American Family Association archives. The photograph itself does not name its subject.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/john-portrait.jpg","dimensions":[2085,2934],"displayBytes":194238,"sourceSha256":"7a1b30d3f9d3ac5c3522b26ae93f556bd6646da9413b3b4a2f13e38ab5936e8b"},"landscape":{"id":"landscape","title":"How this landscape was made","citation":"Geography: docs/research-intake/alford-story-2026-09-22/atlas/alford-atlas.geojson, web/atlas-data.json, PLAT-READINGS.md §§1–5. The river’s disconnected source segments remain disconnected. Family connections retain the source qualifications in the notes above.","refs":"Geography: docs/research-intake/alford-story-2026-09-22/atlas/alford-atlas.geojson, web/atlas-data.json, PLAT-READINGS.md §§1–5. The river’s disconnected source segments remain disconnected. Family connections retain the source qualifications in the notes above.","image":null,"body":"The map combines the held modern river trace with historical tracts reconstructed in the project atlas. Positions derive from the 1851 township plat fitted to the modern township outline. Small lot boundaries are approximate, generally about ±50 metres. No houses or travelled routes have been invented.\n\nThe atlas measures about 3.4 miles in a straight line from the centre of Jacob’s claim to Seaborn’s homestead. The main text rounds this to about three and a half miles. The land spans several sections of the same township.\n\nJohn’s and Seaborn’s colours distinguish generations. The broken boundary around Jacob’s claim accompanies a possible earlier family connection. Outlines accumulate as dated land records; they do not establish uninterrupted possession or inheritance of one tract.\n\nImages shown in the passage are held historical records; texture, highlighting and typography provide the presentation.","html":"<p>The map combines the held modern river trace with historical tracts reconstructed in the project atlas. Positions derive from the 1851 township plat fitted to the modern township outline. Small lot boundaries are approximate, generally about ±50 metres. No houses or travelled routes have been invented.<\/p>\n<p>The atlas measures about 3.4 miles in a straight line from the centre of Jacob’s claim to Seaborn’s homestead. The main text rounds this to about three and a half miles. The land spans several sections of the same township.<\/p>\n<p>John’s and Seaborn’s colours distinguish generations. The broken boundary around Jacob’s claim accompanies a possible earlier family connection. Outlines accumulate as dated land records; they do not establish uninterrupted possession or inheritance of one tract.<\/p>\n<p>Images shown in the passage are held historical records; texture, highlighting and typography provide the presentation.<\/p>"},"escoMarriage":{"id":"escoMarriage","title":"Esco Alford’s marriages","citation":"Compiled Louisiana marriage indexes: Esco Alford and Mary L. Fussell, 28 December 1899; Esco S. Alford and Lynnie Schilling, 30 April 1946; Esco Alford and Florence Bennett, 23 May 1951. Lynnie death evidence: S-1828.","refs":"S-1842; S-1828; C-186","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The marriage is indexed under both spouses. This is an index-level date, not a reading of an original licence. Mary Lou’s death-index parents are Henry T. Fussell and Eliza Hobbs; the story does not extend Eliza’s parentage.\n\nThe compiled marriage indexes also record Esco and Lynnie Schilling on 30 April 1946, and Esco and Florence Bennett on 23 May 1951. Lynnie’s 1947 death is documented separately (S-1828). These later marriages can explain the reference to Mrs. E. S. Alford after Mary Lou’s death; they do not change Christine’s maternity. Original marriage returns have not been read.","html":"<p>The marriage is indexed under both spouses. This is an index-level date, not a reading of an original licence. Mary Lou’s death-index parents are Henry T. Fussell and Eliza Hobbs; the story does not extend Eliza’s parentage.<\/p>\n<p>The compiled marriage indexes also record Esco and Lynnie Schilling on 30 April 1946, and Esco and Florence Bennett on 23 May 1951. Lynnie’s 1947 death is documented separately (S-1828). These later marriages can explain the reference to Mrs. E. S. Alford after Mary Lou’s death; they do not change Christine’s maternity. Original marriage returns have not been read.<\/p>"},"plat":{"id":"plat","title":"The 1851 township map","citation":"Archive: atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §§1–5; 1851-cabell-dependent-resurvey-document-6016369610341538134.pdf in the Section 54 package’s sources/pre-1939-maps/glo directory. Map placement is approximate, generally about ±50 metres.","refs":"Archive: atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §§1–5; 1851-cabell-dependent-resurvey-document-6016369610341538134.pdf in the Section 54 package’s sources/pre-1939-maps/glo directory. Map placement is approximate, generally about ±50 metres.","image":"assets/plat-1851.jpg","caption":"Township 1 South, Range 10 East · 1851 General Land Office plat","body":"The 1851 Cabell dependent resurvey plat divides the township into irregular private claims and smaller numbered lots. Its lot numbers and acreages allow later Alford patents to be placed.\n\nThe image used here is the held atlas rendition of the township drawing. The archived source is the General Land Office plat approved 3 September 1851. The complete source and the georeferencing notes remain in the project archive.","html":"<p>The 1851 Cabell dependent resurvey plat divides the township into irregular private claims and smaller numbered lots. Its lot numbers and acreages allow later Alford patents to be placed.<\/p>\n<p>The image used here is the held atlas rendition of the township drawing. The archived source is the General Land Office plat approved 3 September 1851. The complete source and the georeferencing notes remain in the project archive.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/plat-1851.jpg","dimensions":[1800,1801],"displayBytes":435663,"sourceSha256":"19552f2b549148c20629907c1b38d1d7aea15ea13b0ecbd157d00a01747f3372"},"directory":{"id":"directory","title":"Christine in the Baton Rouge directories","citation":"1942, page 32; 1943, page 27","refs":"S-1256; C-184","image":null,"url":null,"body":"Both entries identify Christine Alford as a nurse for the Parish Health Unit. The display is a typeset excerpt from the archive’s verified readings; no directory image is presented as an original.","html":"<p>Both entries identify Christine Alford as a nurse for the Parish Health Unit. The display is a typeset excerpt from the archive’s verified readings; no directory image is presented as an original.<\/p>"},"patents":{"id":"patents","title":"John S. Alford’s land patents","citation":"General Land Office · 1858, 1861, 1884","refs":"S-1670; S-1810","image":null,"url":null,"body":"John’s 1858 entries total 57.24 + 36.60 = 93.84 acres. Later entries add 22.28 acres in 1861 and 80.57 in 1884. These are acquisition records; no continuous ownership or later transfer is implied.","html":"<p>John’s 1858 entries total 57.24 + 36.60 = 93.84 acres. Later entries add 22.28 acres in 1861 and 80.57 in 1884. These are acquisition records; no continuous ownership or later transfer is implied.<\/p>"},"escoDraft":{"id":"escoDraft","title":"Esco Seaborn Alford’s draft registration","citation":"12 September 1918 · First World War registration","refs":"S-1292","image":"assets/esco-draft-1918.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Esco’s draft registration · 1918 · registration does not establish service","body":"Esco’s own registration supplies his full name and birth date, 21 May 1878. The card is used here as a later witness to his birth, not evidence of military service.","html":"<p>Esco’s own registration supplies his full name and birth date, 21 May 1878. The card is used here as a later witness to his birth, not evidence of military service.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/esco-draft-1918.jpg","dimensions":[1672,920],"displayBytes":172397,"sourceSha256":"c87350ed992c61b1d3ecc693a22ccc04d8fc6d0dbc7fc94af77b1ca48f77c3e9"},"laura":{"id":"laura","title":"Laura A. Alford’s gravestone","citation":"Seaborn Alford Cemetery · held Find a Grave photograph","refs":"C-191; C-212; C-213; S-1654; S-1826; S-1866","image":"assets/laura-grave.png","url":null,"caption":"Laura A. Alford’s marker · undated photograph via Find a Grave · dates read from a separate transcription","body":"Laura’s weathered gravestone is shown as a photograph of the marker. Its dates cannot be read reliably in this image. A 1965 cemetery transcription gives 25 April 1844–19 March 1903; a controlled family Bible abstract also reports her birth date.\n\nSeaborn’s 1919 obituary calls his first wife Laura A. Ellzy and dates their marriage to 24 December 1866. Her identification with John and Elizabeth Ellzey’s daughter is supported by the linked family records, but is not established.","html":"<p>Laura’s weathered gravestone is shown as a photograph of the marker. Its dates cannot be read reliably in this image. A 1965 cemetery transcription gives 25 April 1844–19 March 1903; a controlled family Bible abstract also reports her birth date.<\/p>\n<p>Seaborn’s 1919 obituary calls his first wife Laura A. Ellzy and dates their marriage to 24 December 1866. Her identification with John and Elizabeth Ellzey’s daughter is supported by the linked family records, but is not established.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/laura-grave.jpg","dimensions":[1152,2048],"displayBytes":235950,"sourceSha256":"fd248cc5cd041f6960231428c0dd43c21fff6e0d1d94258383cb4f811146aa1f"},"escoParentage":{"id":"escoParentage","title":"The 1880 family household","citation":"Washington Parish · Seaborn L. and Laura Alford","refs":"C-190; C-191","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The household names two-year-old “Escoe” as Seaborn’s son. Esco’s father is Established in the archive; Laura’s motherhood is Supported through the household and linked family records.","html":"<p>The household names two-year-old “Escoe” as Seaborn’s son. Esco’s father is Established in the archive; Laura’s motherhood is Supported through the household and linked family records.<\/p>"},"escoPortrait":{"id":"escoPortrait","title":"Esco Alford’s newspaper portrait","citation":"The Era-Leader · 24 April 1958, page 1","refs":"S-1806","image":"assets/esco-portrait-caption.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Esco Alford · The Era-Leader · 24 April 1958 · portrait and caption","body":"The hometown paper printed Esco’s photograph and described his agricultural, civic and church work. The portrait used in the story is a held crop of this same image.","html":"<p>The hometown paper printed Esco’s photograph and described his agricultural, civic and church work. The portrait used in the story is a held crop of this same image.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/esco-portrait-caption.jpg","dimensions":[405,1160],"displayBytes":93863,"sourceSha256":"dc6bd04a5e809645c0948a921de31bdb4ca521af25ecc5249130fc288f61c876"},"nursing":{"id":"nursing","title":"Christine’s 1941 graduation notice","citation":"The Bogalusa News, 29 August 1941, printed Page Ten, “Mt. Hermon Events.”","refs":"S-1017; C-112","image":"assets/nursing-1941.jpg","url":null,"caption":"“Mt. Hermon Events” · The Bogalusa News · 29 August 1941 · Page Ten","body":"The notice reports Christine’s graduation from Touro Infirmary’s school of nursing on 20 August 1941. It says she was at home for a three-week vacation before returning to nurse at Touro. The following item reports Esco’s trip to New Orleans to bring his daughter home.\n\nThe notice calls her the daughter of “Mr. and Mrs. Asco Alford.” The next item spells her father’s name “Esco.” The printed page is Page Ten; the saved provider file uses a different image sequence number.","html":"<p>The notice reports Christine’s graduation from Touro Infirmary’s school of nursing on 20 August 1941. It says she was at home for a three-week vacation before returning to nurse at Touro. The following item reports Esco’s trip to New Orleans to bring his daughter home.<\/p>\n<p>The notice calls her the daughter of “Mr. and Mrs. Asco Alford.” The next item spells her father’s name “Esco.” The printed page is Page Ten; the saved provider file uses a different image sequence number.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/nursing-1941.jpg","dimensions":[2716,3769],"displayBytes":330201,"sourceSha256":"b1f0b6f898890935f49ea4c9fbf31bf655b8161517fd7317157a6a4d60d05aee"},"twin":{"id":"twin","title":"Seaborn J. Alford’s obituary","citation":"New Orleans Christian Advocate · 12 June 1884","refs":"C-140; S-1134; S-1579","image":"assets/seaborn-obituary-1884.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Seaborn J. Alford’s obituary · 1884 · New Orleans Christian Advocate","body":"A later account of the 1807 twins’ birth near modern Osyka. It names their father as Jacob, says he came from South Carolina, and leaves their mother unnamed. The archive supports identifying John S., the surviving twin, with John Seaborn of this line.","html":"<p>A later account of the 1807 twins’ birth near modern Osyka. It names their father as Jacob, says he came from South Carolina, and leaves their mother unnamed. The archive supports identifying John S., the surviving twin, with John Seaborn of this line.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/seaborn-obituary-1884.jpg","dimensions":[650,1700],"displayBytes":300959,"sourceSha256":"ef5febb8941470b008867e5ec66a52b1abc2a0653cf35a2534edb78cbe5efb3e"},"survey":{"id":"survey","title":"Survey for the heirs of Jacob Alford","citation":"20 January 1831 · Bogue Chitto · certificate 41","refs":"C-170; S-1227; S-1135","image":"assets/jacob-heirs-survey-1831.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Survey for Jacob Alford’s heirs · 20 January 1831 · FamilySearch","body":"The original instrument identifies the heirs of Jacob Alford and records 636 acres and 54 hundredths. Its drawing shows the river along the claim’s eastern side. The heirs are not individually named.\n\nThis claimant may have been the Jacob named as the twins’ father in the later family account. That identity, and the connection to the North Carolina Jacob, remain open.\n\nThe map outline comes from the archive’s later Section 54 reconstruction, placed on modern river geography. The original drawing is shown separately; it has not been stretched to fit the modern river.","html":"<p>The original instrument identifies the heirs of Jacob Alford and records 636 acres and 54 hundredths. Its drawing shows the river along the claim’s eastern side. The heirs are not individually named.<\/p>\n<p>This claimant may have been the Jacob named as the twins’ father in the later family account. That identity, and the connection to the North Carolina Jacob, remain open.<\/p>\n<p>The map outline comes from the archive’s later Section 54 reconstruction, placed on modern river geography. The original drawing is shown separately; it has not been stretched to fit the modern river.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/jacob-heirs-survey-1831.jpg","dimensions":[2555,3788],"displayBytes":277716,"sourceSha256":"71afa006ca4b8d83d1e049410d59bfbc75b75fcacf942d9e69c54be9ca8c9104"},"field":{"id":"field","title":"The field before the patent","citation":"Archive: atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §§3, 5; S-1810, John S. Alford document 1717; S-1612 (1850 household). The documentary crop retains the neighbouring claim boundary and the lot’s acreage for context.","refs":"Archive: atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §§3, 5; S-1810, John S. Alford document 1717; S-1612 (1850 household). The documentary crop retains the neighbouring claim boundary and the lot’s acreage for context.","image":"assets/plat-1851.jpg","body":"The plat marks FIELD beside the boundary of the older Elisha Roberts claim. The field outline extends into the 36.60-acre lot numbered 1 in section 18. John S. Alford’s 1858 patent includes that lot.\n\nThe notation shows worked ground. It does not name the person farming it in 1851. John is recorded in the parish by 1850, but that alone does not identify him as this field’s farmer.","html":"<p>The plat marks FIELD beside the boundary of the older Elisha Roberts claim. The field outline extends into the 36.60-acre lot numbered 1 in section 18. John S. Alford’s 1858 patent includes that lot.<\/p>\n<p>The notation shows worked ground. It does not name the person farming it in 1851. John is recorded in the parish by 1850, but that alone does not identify him as this field’s farmer.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/plat-1851.jpg","dimensions":[1800,1801],"displayBytes":435663,"sourceSha256":"19552f2b549148c20629907c1b38d1d7aea15ea13b0ecbd157d00a01747f3372"},"escoObit":{"id":"escoObit","title":"Esco’s funeral in the hometown paper","citation":"The Era-Leader · 17 April 1958, page 1","refs":"S-1805; C-112","image":"assets/esco-obituary-1958.jpg","url":null,"body":"The issue dates the Tuesday death to 15 April 1958. It describes a retired farmer and dairyman, names Christine Knight of Baton Rouge as a daughter, and places burial at Bogue Chitto church cemetery.\n\nThe calendar date 15 April is inferred from the notice’s “Tuesday” and the 17 April 1958 issue, rather than printed as a date in the notice.","html":"<p>The issue dates the Tuesday death to 15 April 1958. It describes a retired farmer and dairyman, names Christine Knight of Baton Rouge as a daughter, and places burial at Bogue Chitto church cemetery.<\/p>\n<p>The calendar date 15 April is inferred from the notice’s “Tuesday” and the 17 April 1958 issue, rather than printed as a date in the notice.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/esco-obituary-1958.jpg","dimensions":[580,1350],"displayBytes":164530,"sourceSha256":"be41533ae56d53064b42c21a3d4a0fe58b28c40a412d6be5471955b68618ccf0"},"portHudsonWorks":{"id":"portHudsonWorks","title":"Confederate fortifications at Port Hudson","citation":"McPherson & Oliver · July 1863–1865 · Library of Congress","refs":"LOC 2015647754 · LC-DIG-stereo-1s04331","image":"assets/port-hudson-fortifications.jpg","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2015647754/","caption":"Port Hudson fortifications · July 1863–1865 · McPherson & Oliver / Library of Congress","body":"The Library of Congress dates this photograph of damaged Confederate fortifications to between July 1863 and 1865. The scene shows timber, sandbags and excavated earthworks. It provides context for the siege; no connection to Seaborn’s particular position is established. The page displays a detail of the left stereoscopic view; the viewer shows the complete card. Rights advisory: No known restrictions on publication.","html":"<p>The Library of Congress dates this photograph of damaged Confederate fortifications to between July 1863 and 1865. The scene shows timber, sandbags and excavated earthworks. It provides context for the siege; no connection to Seaborn’s particular position is established. The page displays a detail of the left stereoscopic view; the viewer shows the complete card. Rights advisory: No known restrictions on publication.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/port-hudson-fortifications.jpg","dimensions":[5504,3017],"displayBytes":160595,"sourceSha256":"11309fa36a8e38d4f21911ec22974514e9dec33d578c2c0410820040796b5250"},"parentage":{"id":"parentage","title":"John and Margaret → Seaborn L.","citation":"1860 household; family Bible copy; later family account","refs":"C-209; S-1643; S-1132","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The archive grades this relationship Supported. The 1860 household includes Seaborne L., age 16, with John and Margaret. A Bible copy and a later account of his naming strengthen the identification. The 1850 household has conflicting ages and is not used by itself to prove parentage.","html":"<p>The archive grades this relationship Supported. The 1860 household includes Seaborne L., age 16, with John and Margaret. A Bible copy and a later account of his naming strengthen the identification. The 1850 household has conflicting ages and is not used by itself to prove parentage.<\/p>"},"touroPhoto":{"id":"touroPhoto","title":"Touro Infirmary, circa 1910","citation":"Detroit Publishing Co. · circa 1910 · Library of Congress via Wikimedia Commons","refs":"LOC det.4a23672 · Commons TouroInfirmary1910.jpg","image":"assets/touro-infirmary-c1910.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TouroInfirmary1910.jpg","caption":"Touro Infirmary · circa 1910 · Detroit Publishing Co. / Library of Congress via Wikimedia Commons","body":"This building view shows Touro Infirmary on Prytania Street in New Orleans. Wikimedia Commons dates it circa 1910 and identifies the Library of Congress source as det.4a23672. It predates Christine’s 1941 graduation by about three decades and is shown as historical context. No person or nursing class in the photograph is identified with Christine. Commons marks the photograph public domain in the United States.","html":"<p>This building view shows Touro Infirmary on Prytania Street in New Orleans. Wikimedia Commons dates it circa 1910 and identifies the Library of Congress source as det.4a23672. It predates Christine’s 1941 graduation by about three decades and is shown as historical context. No person or nursing class in the photograph is identified with Christine. Commons marks the photograph public domain in the United States.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/touro-infirmary-c1910.jpg","dimensions":[4200,3354],"displayBytes":285124,"sourceSha256":"584d080e11e0c0c86e6d1e6935022eacf07625a930011be8eded7ddd6eb7a55e"},"marriage":{"id":"marriage","title":"Christine Alford and Charles B. Knight","citation":"Louisiana marriage index · 1 February 1946 · Bienville Parish","refs":"C-113; S-1021","image":"assets/marriage-1946.jpg","url":null,"body":"The indexed marriage identifies the couple and date. The original licence and return have not been reached. Other held records establish Charles B. as Charles Binford Knight.","html":"<p>The indexed marriage identifies the couple and date. The original licence and return have not been reached. Other held records establish Charles B. as Charles Binford Knight.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/marriage-1946.jpg","dimensions":[1920,952],"displayBytes":207930,"sourceSha256":"4943019d6ac961a9685117de958b4923b289ff855bc9d78ecca7070c6f8ea91b"},"deed":{"id":"deed","title":"Jacob Alford’s deed","citation":"Franklin County, North Carolina · 1789 · deed book 7, page 41","refs":"C-139","image":"assets/jacob-deed-1789.jpg","url":null,"caption":"Franklin County deed · 1789 · FamilySearch","body":"Jacob identifies himself as the son of the deceased Julius Alford. This establishes the North Carolina relationship.","html":"<p>Jacob identifies himself as the son of the deceased Julius Alford. This establishes the North Carolina relationship.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/jacob-deed-1789.jpg","dimensions":[4400,3050],"displayBytes":428365,"sourceSha256":"3fc82cdd13822ab6f7775a4bd30d19366fff67fa964a2f2a99cced52e33e31e7"},"memorial":{"id":"memorial","title":"Christine Alford Knight’s memorial","citation":"Obituary clipping preserved in AAFA member file 0741 · 2008","refs":"AAFA-0741-and-John-Seaborn-recovery-2026-08-27; C-112; C-208","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The obituary names parents Esco Seaborn Alford and Mary Lou Fussell, a 1941 Touro nursing degree, a 1944 CPH qualification from Peabody College, 33 years with the Louisiana Public Health Department, and retirement in 1975. These later recollections are attributed as such. Living survivors are omitted. Exact death-day variants remain unresolved; only the year 2008 is used.\n\nOnly selected biographical facts are presented. The complete obituary, which names living descendants, is not reproduced.","html":"<p>The obituary names parents Esco Seaborn Alford and Mary Lou Fussell, a 1941 Touro nursing degree, a 1944 CPH qualification from Peabody College, 33 years with the Louisiana Public Health Department, and retirement in 1975. These later recollections are attributed as such. Living survivors are omitted. Exact death-day variants remain unresolved; only the year 2008 is used.<\/p>\n<p>Only selected biographical facts are presented. The complete obituary, which names living descendants, is not reproduced.<\/p>"},"john":{"id":"john","title":"John’s lots on the map","citation":"Archive: S-1670; S-1810; atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §3. GLO documents 1718 (57.24 acres) and 1717 (36.60 acres), 1 June 1858; subsequent entry, 10 May 1861. The displayed image is the township plat used to locate the lots.","refs":"Archive: S-1670; S-1810; atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §3. GLO documents 1718 (57.24 acres) and 1717 (36.60 acres), 1 June 1858; subsequent entry, 10 May 1861. The displayed image is the township plat used to locate the lots.","image":"assets/plat-1851.jpg","body":"John S. Alford’s first 1858 entry is section 17, lots 1 and 5: 25.80 + 31.44 = 57.24 acres. The second is section 18, lot 1: 36.60 acres. Together they total 93.84 acres.\n\nThe green outlines follow the archive’s reconstruction from the 1851 plat and modern section geometry. Separate pieces of land are kept separate. Patent dates identify acquisition records; retaining their outlines later in the story does not assert continuous ownership.\n\nIn the 1881 comparison, the map also includes John’s 22.28-acre entry of 10 May 1861, section 17, lot 6.","html":"<p>John S. Alford’s first 1858 entry is section 17, lots 1 and 5: 25.80 + 31.44 = 57.24 acres. The second is section 18, lot 1: 36.60 acres. Together they total 93.84 acres.<\/p>\n<p>The green outlines follow the archive’s reconstruction from the 1851 plat and modern section geometry. Separate pieces of land are kept separate. Patent dates identify acquisition records; retaining their outlines later in the story does not assert continuous ownership.<\/p>\n<p>In the 1881 comparison, the map also includes John’s 22.28-acre entry of 10 May 1861, section 17, lot 6.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/plat-1851.jpg","dimensions":[1800,1801],"displayBytes":435663,"sourceSha256":"19552f2b549148c20629907c1b38d1d7aea15ea13b0ecbd157d00a01747f3372"},"maryObit":{"id":"maryObit","title":"“Mrs. Lou Alford”","citation":"The Daily News, Bogalusa · 5 February 1946, page 4","refs":"S-1803; S-1814; C-208","image":"assets/mary-lou-obituary-1946.png","url":null,"caption":"Mary Lou’s death notice · Bogalusa Daily News · 5 February 1946 · excerpt","body":"The contemporary notice reports Lou Fussell Alford’s death Monday morning, 4 February. It names husband Esco and daughter Mrs. Christine Knight. This exhibit is an existing archival crop of the notice.\n\nThe image is an assembled excerpt from separate portions of the newspaper column; its blocks are not in normal reading order. The opening is at upper right, followed by the continuation at left.","html":"<p>The contemporary notice reports Lou Fussell Alford’s death Monday morning, 4 February. It names husband Esco and daughter Mrs. Christine Knight. This exhibit is an existing archival crop of the notice.<\/p>\n<p>The image is an assembled excerpt from separate portions of the newspaper column; its blocks are not in normal reading order. The opening is at upper right, followed by the continuation at left.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/mary-lou-obituary-1946.jpg","dimensions":[1450,770],"displayBytes":130869,"sourceSha256":"e9f1d2c8bf1f5f18e62843e2c51ad8b8b94915d5cb5e009c8baecb5ff0c08e16"},"farm":{"id":"farm","title":"1850 agricultural return","citation":"Washington Parish · Schedule 4 · frame 836, row 25","refs":"S-1756","image":null,"url":null,"body":"John Alford’s row records 40 improved and 600 unimproved acres. The farm’s association with this John is a correlation with the population return. The schedule does not locate the farm, establish title, or prove that it is Jacob’s claim.","html":"<p>John Alford’s row records 40 improved and 600 unimproved acres. The farm’s association with this John is a correlation with the population return. The schedule does not locate the farm, establish title, or prove that it is Jacob’s claim.<\/p>"},"census1880":{"id":"census1880","title":"John S. Alford in the 1880 census","citation":"Washington Parish · 1880 federal census · line 31","refs":"S-1613; C-140","image":null,"url":null,"body":"The entry for John S. Alford, 73, gives his own birthplace as Louisiana and both of his parents’ birthplaces as North Carolina. His twin Seaborn J.’s 1880 entry in Pike County, Mississippi gives Virginia for both parents. A census does not say who answered the questions, and neither entry names Jacob. An earlier reading of this page, which gave South Carolina, took the cells from Margaret’s line and was corrected by the archive on 11 September 2026.","html":"<p>The entry for John S. Alford, 73, gives his own birthplace as Louisiana and both of his parents’ birthplaces as North Carolina. His twin Seaborn J.’s 1880 entry in Pike County, Mississippi gives Virginia for both parents. A census does not say who answered the questions, and neither entry names Jacob. An earlier reading of this page, which gave South Carolina, took the cells from Margaret’s line and was corrected by the archive on 11 September 2026.<\/p>"},"homestead":{"id":"homestead","title":"Seaborn’s homestead on the township plat","citation":"Archive: S-1670; atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §2; alford-direct-line-land-tenure-2026-09-08 patent reading. Patent signed 10 February 1881. The displayed plat supplies the lot positions; it predates the patent by thirty years.","refs":"Archive: S-1670; atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §2; alford-direct-line-land-tenure-2026-09-08 patent reading. Patent signed 10 February 1881. The displayed plat supplies the lot positions; it predates the patent by thirty years.","image":"assets/plat-1851.jpg","body":"Seaborn L. Alford’s homestead patent, document 29, names lots 1, 2 and 3 of section 20, Township 1 South, Range 10 East. On the 1851 plat they measure 44.68, 45.12 and 45.44 acres: a total of 135.24.\n\nThese are the northern three lots of the narrow section 20 strip. The archive reconstructs their position using the historical lotting and modern section boundary. The map shows the relationship between land records; neither house has been located.","html":"<p>Seaborn L. Alford’s homestead patent, document 29, names lots 1, 2 and 3 of section 20, Township 1 South, Range 10 East. On the 1851 plat they measure 44.68, 45.12 and 45.44 acres: a total of 135.24.<\/p>\n<p>These are the northern three lots of the narrow section 20 strip. The archive reconstructs their position using the historical lotting and modern section boundary. The map shows the relationship between land records; neither house has been located.<\/p>","display":"assets/display/plat-1851.jpg","dimensions":[1800,1801],"displayBytes":435663,"sourceSha256":"19552f2b549148c20629907c1b38d1d7aea15ea13b0ecbd157d00a01747f3372"},"candacyObit":{"id":"candacyObit","title":"Candacy Alford’s 1955 obituary","citation":"The Era-Leader, Thursday 24 November 1955, page 1, column 6, death notice for Candacy Bankston Alford.","refs":"S-1652; C-210","image":null,"body":"Candacy’s death notice identifies her son Jacob Alford and daughters Frankie Penny and Agnes Haley. It names surviving stepchildren as well. The 1919 obituary of Seaborn reports three children from his marriage to Candacy; the later notice supplies their names.\n\nOnly the relevant family information is summarized here.","html":"<p>Candacy’s death notice identifies her son Jacob Alford and daughters Frankie Penny and Agnes Haley. It names surviving stepchildren as well. The 1919 obituary of Seaborn reports three children from his marriage to Candacy; the later notice supplies their names.<\/p>\n<p>Only the relevant family information is summarized here.<\/p>"}},"parts":{"1":"Early records","2":"John, Margaret, Seaborn & Laura","3":"Esco, Mary Lou & Christine","4":"Education, marriage & work","5":"Christine’s later life"}};
